<http://data.silknow.org/event/e137a168-d353-37b1-9dfc-260d064e90d5>
        a                              ecrm:E8_Acquisition ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Mary embroidered this panel with a number of others during her time of imprisonment in England.  Needlework provided her with a practical occupation and it was also an outlet for her to express the frustation of her situation, her choice of subject matter including many mottoes and emblems representing courage in adversity.  She also depicted various subjects from natural history and in this case has copied an illustration from the 1560 edition of Conrad Gesner's <i>Icones Animalium</i>.  The dark silk with which the ape was embroidered has partially decayed, exposing the drawing out of the design on the canvas ground."@en ;
        ecrm:P14_carried_out_by        <http://data.silknow.org/actor/d858d816-5ef4-3380-8e9e-1aff3f2527db> ;
        ecrm:P22_transferred_title_to  <http://data.silknow.org/actor/f0577f91-f887-3019-bf88-f9e5ba019390> ;
        ecrm:P23_transferred_title_from
                "Presented by Art Fund" ;
        ecrm:P24_transferred_title_of  <http://data.silknow.org/object/0b1dbac2-fc90-3008-aefc-a8755e41b27b> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Mary embroidered this panel with a number of others during her time of imprisonment in England.  Needlework provided her with a practical occupation and it was also an outlet for her to express the frustation of her situation, her choice of subject matter including many mottoes and emblems representing courage in adversity.  She also depicted various subjects from natural history and in this case has copied an illustration from the 1560 edition of Conrad Gesner's <i>Icones Animalium</i>.  The dark silk with which the ape was embroidered has partially decayed, exposing the drawing out of the design on the canvas ground."@en .

